A||||5||Ironhorse||THOSE FRIGGIN SPRINGS||||11||
Z||000000||Ironhorse||06-29-2000||02:03 PM||||I'm 99% done on rebuilding my 2.8 engine for 85GT.  I bought cat back from fiero store and was getting ready to install it... I took my exhaust to cradle springs and I can't get those G*****N F**KING springs in there without nicking my hand, finger, springs flying from one bay to another.  This is one of those few components where I feel like going to GM, grab all their engineers, bring them home, say "redesign this friggin tihng or you'll never be able to design again", and lock them up in the garage.  Is there something else I cna use that easier than springs (no i ain't gonna use coat hanger) or is there a better way to install those springs on there.  BTW the engine/tranny/cradle is not in the car yet||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||06-29-2000||02:18 PM||||maybe the same tools used for installing springs in drum brakes? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-29-2000||02:29 PM||||Everytime I replace springs like that I always promise to go get those brake tools. But, what I usually end up doing, is clamping a hefty pair of Vice-grips onto the spring (allow enough room to hook it into the hole), then muscle it in, usually skinning a knuckle in the process.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||06-29-2000||06:21 PM||||Been there, done that!  I made a "spring installation tool" that works(for me anyway)everytime.  You have to sacrifice a large flat blade screw driver.  Just cut a v notch in the middle of the blade(in the end you normally insert in the screw head)making one tong a little longer than the other.  It has to be fairly deep, maybe 1/2" or so.  Kind of looks like a hand held yard "weeder".<P>    To use it (should be fairly obvious) you insert the spring end into the groove, and it holds it while you stretch the spring into position.  Make sure you have the "long tong" positioned so it prevents the spring from slipping off.  If you use a long handled screw stick, you will never bust another knuckle putting the springs on.<P>     Just a suggestion, based on what works for me.<P>     Good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fret Naught||06-30-2000||01:22 AM||||I just did the exhaust on my 86 SE, I found that in reality, the first two went on pretty easy, it was the last one (rear most springs, three to a bracket) that was a real chore, I took a piece of 3/16 round bar, heated the end with a propane torch, and bent a tight hook in the end. I put a T-Handle on the end the whole tool was about 8" long. then, I hooked the spring to the car, reached in with my hook, grabbed the dangeling end pulled it down and onto the exhaust bracket. It worked real well, and because of the T handle I could really put a lot of force on the tool when I was pulling on the spring. Hope that helps ||||reg||7
Z||000005||terryk||06-30-2000||01:12 PM||||Hey Sage, that's a clever idea. I'll have to try it. <P>I've been using a fence stretcher and come-along up until now.||||reg||14
